---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {
  models,
  providers,
  updatedAt,
  sourceUrl,
  formatContext,
  formatPrice,
} from '../lib/models';

const exact = models.filter((m) => m.counting === 'exact');
const tiered = models.filter((m) => m.tier);
const deprecated = models.filter((m) => m.deprecatedOn);
const cacheWrite = models.filter((m) => m.cacheWrite);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'How Price Per Token counts tokens and prices models',
  dateModified: updatedAt,
  description:
    'Methodology behind the token counts, price figures and cost estimates: which counts are exact, what the prices exclude, and where the data comes from.',
};
---

<Base
  title="Methodology — how these token counts and prices work"
  description={`Where Price Per Token's pricing comes from, which token counts are exact, and what the cost estimates exclude. ${models.length} models, synced ${updatedAt}.`}
  breadcrumbs={[['Methodology', '/methodology']]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="wrap page">
    <span class="eyebrow">Updated {updatedAt}</span>
    <h1>How these numbers work</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A price comparator is only worth reading if you know what it is measuring
      and where it stops being precise. This page is that boundary, written out.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the prices come from</h2>
    <p>
      Rates are pulled from the
      <a href={sourceUrl} rel="nofollow noopener">LiteLLM pricing dataset</a>, an
      open, community-maintained record of published provider rates, and
      normalised into USD per million tokens. The sync runs as a script, not by
      hand, so a stale figure is a missed cron job rather than a forgotten edit.
      Every page carries the sync date; the build fails if the data is more than
      30 days old.
    </p>
    <p>
      Currently {models.length} models across {providers.length} providers. Only
      first-party rates are listed. Resellers and inference platforms — Bedrock,
      Azure, OpenRouter, Fireworks, Together — often price the same model
      differently, and mixing them into one table makes it look like a model has
      several prices.
    </p>

    <h2>Which token counts are exact</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>{exact.length} models are counted exactly.</strong> OpenAI publishes
      its tokenizer, so your text is run through the real encoder — the same
      segmentation the API bills against.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every other family is <strong>estimated</strong>. Anthropic, Google,
      Mistral, xAI, Moonshot and the rest do not publish a tokenizer, so the
      count is the OpenAI count scaled by a published ratio for that family.
      Expect roughly ±10%, and more on code or non-English text, where
      tokenizers diverge most.
    </p>
    <p>
      This distinction is on every model, as an <span class="badge badge--exact">exact</span>
      or <span class="badge badge--estimated">estimated</span> badge. Several
      comparable tools apply the OpenAI tokenizer to all models without saying
      so, which reads as precision it does not have.
    </p>

    <h2>Long-context rates</h2>
    <p>
      {tiered.length} models charge a higher rate once a single request passes a
      context threshold. When your prompt crosses it, the higher rate is applied
      automatically and the row is flagged — you do not have to know the
      threshold exists to avoid being misled by the base figure.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Model</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Base in</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Threshold</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Above</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {tiered.slice(0, 10).map((m) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={`/models/${m.slug}`}>{m.name}</a></td>
              <td class="num">{formatPrice(m.input)}</td>
              <td class="num">{formatContext(m.tier!.threshold)}</td>
              <td class="num">{formatPrice(m.tier!.input)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What the cost estimate excludes</h2>
    <ul class="excl">
      <li>Batch and off-peak discounts</li>
      <li>Negotiated or enterprise rates</li>
      <li>Free tiers and trial credits</li>
      <li>Reasoning tokens billed beyond the visible output on thinking models</li>
      <li>Tool calls, web search, and other per-request surcharges</li>
      <li>Retries, which are invisible until the invoice arrives</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Cache pricing is included where published: read rates for every model that
      has one, and separate write rates for the {cacheWrite.length} models that
      bill cache creation on its own — Anthropic charges it at two TTLs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Output length is an input you choose, not a measurement. The presets scale
      it from the prompt because that is the closest honest proxy; a real
      workload is the only way to know.
    </p>

    <h2>Deprecation</h2>
    <p>
      {deprecated.length} listed models have a published deprecation date. They
      are hidden from the picker by default and marked on provider tables, since
      pricing one into a plan you will still be running next year is a trap.
    </p>

    <h2>What we would get wrong first</h2>
    <p>
      Provider pages change without notice, and the upstream dataset can lag a
      price change by days. The counts for non-OpenAI families are the softest
      number here — if a decision turns on a difference of a few percent, count
      your real payload against the provider's own token-counting endpoint
      instead of trusting this page.
    </p>

    <p class="links">
      <a href="/">Price a prompt →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: 3rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .excl {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .excl li {
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .links {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
